argin": 3.6, "analyst_adjustment": 0.6, "key_factors": ["A strongly favorable midterm environment for Democrats offsets the district's R+3 lean and the incumbent's advantage", "All ten expert raters place the race at Tossup or better for Cooke, with none favoring Van Orden", "Prediction markets on Kalshi and Polymarket price Cooke near a three-in-four favorite", "Cooke has outraised Van Orden, though his cash-on-hand edge is large and the only polls are Democratic-sponsored, showing her up one to four"], "rationale": "The fundamentals combine a Republican-leaning district and incumbency with a strongly anti-incumbent-party environment, and the qualitative evidence points the same way: every rater grades the race Tossup to Likely D for Cooke, and markets price her around 74 percent. Available surveys, while all from Democratic sponsors, consistently show Cooke narrowly ahead, justifying a small Democratic tilt beyond the fundamentals alone.", "watch": "Van Orden's large cash-on-hand advantage, late Republican outside spending, or any improvement in the national environment for Republicans could erase Cooke's modest edge.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Derrick Van Orden (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Derrick Van Orden is seeking a third term in a western Wisconsin district that leans about three points Republican, facing Democrat Rebecca Cooke in a rematch of their close 2024 contest. The national midterm environment favors Democrats this cycle, and Cooke has outraised the incumbent, though Van Orden enters the final stretch with a large cash-on-hand lead. The limited polling available, all commissioned by Democratic sponsors, shows Cooke narrowly ahead and warrants some caution.\n\nEvery major political rater places the race at Tossup or better for Democrats, with several calling it a Democratic flip, and prediction markets price Cooke as roughly a thr